#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084331} :activity/id #uuid "4c50d575-0f8a-592f-8bee-9a211447ec98" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847 :topic/id #uuid "6b5cc7c5-f3ba-5a78-aceb-e5c263dbe224" :topic/math-academy-id 765} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084412 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the isosceles right triangle whose legs are both equal to $1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d1e8bd928fe666859f7101be65819af5f16fe81df3900941650d279eecd496.png)\n\nBy considering either of the $45^\\circ$ angles in this triangle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\[5pt] \\cos 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{adj.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\[5pt] \\tan 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{adj.}} = \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsosceles right triangles are very common, so the values above occur a lot in trigonometry. It's a good idea to remember them." :tutorial/id #uuid "43cdd368-7207-5d18-8f22-5e384ebbc118" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2457 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "76d3dfad-1625-5612-88e9-44f6df730e85" :step/math-academy-id 453556 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084332}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083364 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082956 :question/id #uuid "58cfd10d-afd2-5678-a38a-e0fbc0221076" :question/math-academy-id "e-3272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a622bad-6820-5e34-a347-890709103bc0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049915}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083829}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 45 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "2f3823b3-f0cb-5b53-ab96-dc48e9c09690" :step/math-academy-id 453557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084413 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle whose shortest leg has a length equal to $1$, shown below.\n\n![](images/67/679ce670484e57ba699030de4cf6d9ba52a5d6910d22b4beb2ad5d4e8ac4e0e0.png)\n\nNow, we can calculate some trigonometric ratios without a calculator. Let's start with the $30^\\circ$-angle. By the definition of sine, cosine, and tangent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 30^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{hyp.} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\\n\\cos 30^{∘} &= \\frac{adj.}{hyp.} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\\n\\tan 30^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{adj.} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the $60^\\circ$-angle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 60^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{hyp.} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\\n\\cos 60^{∘} &= \\frac{adj.}{hyp.} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\\n\\tan 60^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{adj.} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d87688da-9a24-5f3e-9160-de9562217a11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2460 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Ratios for 30-60-90 Triangles"} :step/id #uuid "60a44c52-c126-53ea-b949-1b44b8614b1c" :step/math-academy-id 453561 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083365 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082957 :question/id #uuid "b593adba-7766-5cce-b607-ae3f2ad24970" :question/math-academy-id "e-3275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "462eeb28-d618-5c29-8568-186d63645f99" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048737}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083828}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 30 and 60 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "bf2f2513-8031-5383-8527-049ce8fb6c88" :step/math-academy-id 453562 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084414 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $0^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\sin \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n\nWhy is this so? If we look at the right triangle below, we can imagine making the angle $\\theta$ smaller by moving the vertex $C$ towards $A$.\n\n![](images/9b/9bee0468c989067e12648e538788bcd8c7fb879e112000a942a1ee6f41d95c7d.png)\n\nAs the angle $\\theta$ gets closer and closer to zero, the opposite side $AC$ also approaches zero. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the adjacent side $AB$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches zero (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 0^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAC \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AB\n$$\n\nTherefore, when\n\n$$\n\\theta = 0^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (0^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} = 0, \\\\\n\\cos (0^{∘}) &= \\frac{AB}{BC} = 1, \\\\\n\\tan (0^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{AB} = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d78c0611-a7ea-5bc0-830b-fb04311281da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2461 :tutorial/title "Special Values of the Zero Degree Angle"} :step/id #uuid "34c6a603-c08d-5f28-95f6-03d5cf07c17e" :step/math-academy-id 453566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083366 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082958 :question/id #uuid "0959222f-542e-5cea-b10a-fa3872c93571" :question/math-academy-id "e-3278"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59c7be47-27c1-5300-8fd9-0e7d39e977d9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083652}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 0 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "efdf3091-c5e4-5290-a97f-f9040128b097" :step/math-academy-id 453567 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084415 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $90^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\sin \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | Undefined |\n\nTo understand where the values come from, this time, we can imagine increasing the value of the angle $\\theta$ by moving the vertex $B$ towards $A$.\n\n![](images/53/53d538ce2eca6af579b556bdac72d9fba9ba5e817de38ff73236a312b401c4cf.png)\n\nAs $B$ moves towards $A$, the adjacent side $AB$ gets very close to zero, and the angle $\\theta$ gets very close to $90^\\circ$. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the opposite side $AC$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches $90^\\circ$ (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 90^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAB \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AC\n$$\n\nTherefore, when\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (90^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} = 1, \\\\\n\\cos (90^{∘}) &= \\frac{AB}{BC} = 0, \\\\\n\\tan (90^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{AB} = \\frac{AC}{0} = undefined\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when calculating the tangent, we get a $0$ in the denominator. We know that we can't divide by zero. Therefore, the tangent of a right angle is said to be undefined." :tutorial/id #uuid "c602245b-ade3-5bc6-8703-b73dd0425cba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2462 :tutorial/title "Special Values of the 90 Degree Angle"} :step/id #uuid "d2d14fdc-c970-5852-b625-d0487c11289e" :step/math-academy-id 453570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083367 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082959 :question/id #uuid "bac66691-92d3-56d4-84a1-7502ba095e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-3281"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a330418-1f66-5dfa-9543-8c0d5ec6e8f2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083654}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 90 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "4c9fa83a-1c50-5837-8af1-69e0fc1506ac" :step/math-academy-id 453571}] :activity/title "Special Trigonometric Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083454}]}
